---
title: "Counts and metadata"
description: >
  Turn a GEO accession into a counts matrix in R, join the sample metadata,
  and carry both into Seurat for SCTransform and UMAP.
output: rmarkdown::html_document
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  eval = TRUE,
  fig.path = "img/"
)
```

The other vignette stops at the metadata. This one goes the rest of the way:
from an accession to a counts matrix, with the per-cell annotation the
submitter deposited and the study metadata seqout.org holds, ready to hand to
Seurat.

Reading counts needs `Matrix` for the sparse formats and `hdf5r` for `.h5` and
`.h5ad`. Both are suggested rather than required, so a plain install stays
light:

```{r install, eval=FALSE}
install.packages(c("Matrix", "hdf5r"))
```

```{r setup}
library(seqout)

con <- seqout_connect()
ACCESSION <- "GSE297547" # PBMCs from myasthenia gravis patients and controls
```

## Fetching counts form GEO 

`seqout_counts()` resolves and groups files but downloads nothing. GEO
supplementary payloads run to tens of gigabytes, so the manifest comes first
and costs only the API calls that list the files.

A unit is a group of files that read as one matrix: a 10x triplet
(`matrix.mtx` with `barcodes.tsv` and `features.tsv`), a CellRanger `.h5`, an
`.h5ad`, an `.rds`, or a delimited table.

```{r manifest}
counts <- seqout_counts(con, ACCESSION)
m <- manifest(counts)

cat(nrow(m), "units\n")
head(m[, c("unit", "sample", "format", "preferred", "has_metadata")], 8)
```

`preferred` marks the unit that `seqout_matrix()` picks for each sample. A
submitter often deposits the same counts twice, for instance a 10x `.h5`
alongside the mtx triplet it was built from. Both are listed; the losing one
stays reachable by its label so nothing is hidden from you.

`has_metadata` says whether per-cell annotation is available at all, either as
a sidecar file or embedded in the container. `.h5ad` and `.rds` always carry
their own, which is why every unit here reports `TRUE`.

```{r formats}
table(m$format)
```

## Reading one sample

Picking a single unit keeps this vignette fast. `seqout_matrix()` downloads
that unit's files, caches them under `tools::R_user_dir("seqout", "cache")`,
and reads them.

```{r read-rna}
rna <- seqout_matrix(counts, sample = seqout_units(counts)[[1]]$label)
rna
```

A `seqout_matrix` is deliberately in AnnData orientation: rows are
observations, columns are genes. For single-cell data a row is a cell; for bulk
it is a biological sample. That is what lets one type cover both without the
caller checking which they have.

```{r shape}
dim(rna$X)
class(rna$X)
```

```{r counts-matrix}
dim(counts_matrix(rna))
```

`kind` is decided on evidence. Barcode-looking row labels mean single-cell; row
labels that are GSM accessions, or an observation count no larger than the
number of samples in the series, mean bulk. A dense table with a few hundred
columns could be a Smart-seq plate or a large bulk study, and in that band
`kind` reports `unknown`. Check `evidence` when the answer matters.

```{r evidence}
rna$kind
rna$evidence
```

## Cell-level metadata

`obs` carries whatever per-cell annotation the submitter deposited. For an
`.h5ad` or `.rds` that comes from inside the container; for a 10x unit it comes
from a sidecar file, joined on the cell label.

```{r obs-rna}
lapply(rna$obs, function(x) head(unique(as.character(x)), 3))
```

Column names differ wildly between submitters. This one named their annotation
`type1`, `type2`, `type3`, which carries no vocabulary a matcher could key on,
so nothing here claims a particular column is the cell type. The columns are
still there and still usable; `type3` is the donor code and `type1` the donor
group, both recorded per cell.

Gene names are worth the same check before you plan an analysis. This submitter
deposited them anonymised, which puts marker-based cell typing out of reach for
this study.

```{r gene-names}
head(rownames(rna$var), 5)
```

## Picking an assay

This is a CITE-seq study, so every sample ships two matrices: gene expression
and an antibody panel. Both are `.h5ad`, so nothing in the file type separates
them, and the antibody file happens to sort first alphabetically.

`seqout_counts()` defaults to `assay = "rna"` for that reason. Ask for the
panel explicitly and you get it, with its own annotation.

```{r read-adt}
adt <- seqout_counts(con, ACCESSION, assay = "adt")
adt_m <- seqout_matrix(adt, sample = seqout_units(adt)[[1]]$label)

dim(adt_m$X)
rownames(adt_m$var)
```

```{r celltype}
head(sort(table(adt_m$obs$Cell_Type_Experimental), decreasing = TRUE))
```

## Study metadata from seqout.org

The counts tell you what was measured. They say nothing about who the donors
were, what the condition was, or which paper this came from. That lives in the
API.

```{r project}
p <- project_metadata(con, ACCESSION, transpose = T)
p
```

We can look at the experiment design:

```{r design}
design <- sample_frame(project_samples(con, ACCESSION))
head(design)
```

## Combining counts and sample level metadata

Every cell in a unit is associated with a GSM which has its
own metadata in the design matrix:

```{r join}
design[design$sample == rna$accession, ]
```

The two sources can also be checked against each other, which is worth doing
before trusting either. `type3` and `type1` are the submitter's own per-cell
labels; `design` is what GEO holds.

```{r cross-check}
picked <- c("GSM8994520", "GSM8994523", "GSM8994533")

cells <- do.call(rbind, lapply(picked, function(a) {
  o <- seqout_matrix(counts, sample = a)$obs
  data.frame(sample = a, per_cell = unique(o$type1), donor = unique(o$type3))
}))
merge(cells, design[, c("sample", "title")], by = "sample")
```

The per-cell labels and the donor codes agree.

## Into Seurat

For each sample we now have the sample level metadata and cell level metadata,
we can now run a deeper analyis:

```{r seurat-build, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
library(Seurat)
library(Matrix)
set.seed(1)

n_cells <- 1500

objs <- lapply(picked, function(accession) {
  x <- counts_matrix(seqout_matrix(counts, sample = accession))
  keep <- sample(ncol(x), min(n_cells, ncol(x)))
  covariates <- design[design$sample == accession, ]

  obj <- CreateSeuratObject(
    counts = x[, keep, drop = FALSE],
    project = accession, min.cells = 3, min.features = 200
  )
  obj$sample <- accession
  obj$donor <- sub(".*from ([A-Z]+[0-9]+).*", "\\1", covariates$title)
  obj$group <- ifelse(grepl("^MG", obj$donor), "MG", "control")
  obj$Sex <- covariates$Sex
  obj$age <- as.integer(covariates$age)
  obj
})
```

`counts_matrix()` returns a sparse matrix in the gene x cell format, and is also
exported as `genexcell_counts()`; `cellxgene_counts()` gives the other
orientation. Covariates
from `sample_frame()` are stored as `meta.data` in the Seurat object.

```{r seurat-merge, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
pbmc <- merge(objs[[1]], objs[-1],
  add.cell.ids = vapply(objs, function(o) unique(o$donor), character(1))
)
pbmc[["percent.mt"]] <- PercentageFeatureSet(pbmc, pattern = "^MT-")

table(pbmc$donor, pbmc$group)
```




```{r sctransform, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
pbmc <- SCTransform(pbmc, vars.to.regress = "percent.mt", verbose = FALSE)
pbmc <- RunPCA(pbmc, npcs = 30, verbose = FALSE)
pbmc <- RunUMAP(pbmc, dims = 1:30, verbose = FALSE)
pbmc <- FindNeighbors(pbmc, dims = 1:30, verbose = FALSE)
pbmc <- FindClusters(pbmc, resolution = 0.4, verbose = FALSE)

pbmc
```

```{r umap-clusters, fig.width=6, fig.height=5, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
DimPlot(pbmc, label = TRUE) + ggplot2::ggtitle("Clusters")
```

```{r umap-metadata, fig.width=10, fig.height=4.5, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
DimPlot(pbmc, group.by = c("donor", "group", "Sex"), ncol = 3)
```

```{r composition}
round(prop.table(table(pbmc$seurat_clusters, pbmc$group), margin = 2), 3)
```

## Fetching all samples counts

`matrices()` reads every preferred unit and returns a list keyed by unit label.
It prefetches all the files in one call, because downloading a 19-sample series
one stream at a time is the slow way to do it.

```{r whole-study, eval=FALSE}
mats <- matrices(counts) # every sample
paths <- seqout_counts_files(counts, sample = "GSM8994520") # files, no parsing
```

The download is the expensive part, and a series can be tens of gigabytes.
`matrices()` skips a unit it cannot read, so compare the number of results
against the manifest.

```{r close}
seqout_close(con)
```
